ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [
  "Win region (cache<all-families + heavy load + small B + larger K) is exactly where queue depth permits reordering AND eviction churn dominates — this is a textbook LRU-with-locality result: any policy that increases temporal locality of accesses to a capacity-limited LRU cache reduces miss rate. PFA is essentially 'access-grouping for LRU', a known cache-friendly scheduling pattern (cf. Belady/locality literature, GPU warp-coalescing analogs). The 76% number is impressive but unsurprising given B=4, K=8-16, and one-family cache: PFA approaches the offline-optimal per-family-batch lower bound (~1 prefix-load per family per residency epoch); FCFS approaches ~one prefix-load per arriving family-switch. The ratio is ~ K_active_in_window / batches_per_family, predictable from queue-depth and family count alone.",
  "All-families-fit -> 0% is not a 'pre-registered honest negative confirming the mechanism' so much as a tautology: with no evictions, admission order cannot affect miss count. This is arithmetic, not a mechanism test.",
  "Unbounded-W +17% with 1.29-1.34x p99 is the standard SJF/locality-vs-FCFS fairness/throughput tradeoff (Little's-law-adjacent); the 'bounded W keeps most of the win' result is the well-known aging/bounded-bypass mitigation used in OS schedulers for 50+ years."
]
CONFOUNDS: [
  "Constant service time isolates 'admission order' but also REMOVES the realistic confound where prefix-cache hits shorten prefill and thus service time — which on real hardware feeds back into queue depth and changes the fairness tradeoff. The sim's clean separation may overstate matched-fairness feasibility.",
  "Block-pinning of in-flight requests + FREE resident blocks is an idealized cache model: no partial-block costs, no allocator fragmentation, no paged-attention block contention with scratch/KV growth. On real vLLM/SGLang the prefill-token proxy is not monotone with wall-clock TTFT.",
  "Matched-fairness threshold (p99 within 10% of FCFS) is a researcher-chosen knob; achieved ratio 1.018-1.019 is FAR inside the budget, suggesting the bounded-W sweep grid {0,1,2,4,8,16,32,inf} may not be probing the fairness frontier tightly (the binding constraint is grid coarseness, not the policy).",
  "Zipf s=1.0 'moderate' skew + uniform are tested; real agentic traffic is heavier-tailed (few hot system prompts dominate) which would FAVOR even naive FCFS+APC and SHRINK the PFA delta — the strongest wins are at UNIFORM family distribution (K=8/16 uniform), the least realistic regime."
]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [
  "Service time independent of cache-hit state (real prefill cost scales with missed tokens).",
  "Scratch/decode tokens are a 'fixed cost identical across policies' — true only if decode length distribution is policy-independent; under load, longer waits can correlate with request type via family.",
  "No interaction with chunked prefill, priority/SLO classes, speculative decoding — all explicitly deferred but load-bearing for the GPU claim.",
  "6 seeds is the minimum end of the pre-reg ('>=5, use 8'); variance characterization on the 15 winning cells is thin for a 'clean CI excludes 0' claim across 92 multiple comparisons (no FWER/FDR correction reported).",
  "W=inf p99 ratio 1.29-1.34 — was this measured per-cell or aggregated? Distribution of fairness cost across cells unreported."
]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [
  "92 matched cells x 2 metrics, no multiple-comparison correction reported — 15 'CI<0' wins at nominal 95% expected false-positive ~4-5 even under null. Wins concentrate in one regime, which mitigates but does not eliminate; a regime-stratified or Bonferroni/BH-adjusted summary would strengthen.",
  "Paired bootstrap 95% CI reported, but per-cell n=6 seeds is small for bootstrap CI stability (rule of thumb >=20); CI widths likely understate uncertainty especially near zero.",
  "No reported variance of the p99 fairness ratio itself — 'mean ratio 1.018' could hide cells at 1.10 ceiling.",
  "No sensitivity analysis on PREFIX_LEN=2048 / block=16 / CACHE_BLOCKS sizing — the 'few(~K/2)' regime is one point per K, not a curve.",
  "No reported correlation between W-selected-for-matched-fairness and the magnitude of savings — needed to claim the bounded-W mechanism is doing the work vs. grid-search luck."
]
RATIONALE: The L0 result is internally consistent and the matched-fairness control is the right idea, but the headline effect is largely PREDICTED by basic LRU-with-locality theory plus the standard aging-bounded-bypass scheduling pattern — not a surprising emergent phenomenon. The 0%-when-cache-fits result is arithmetic, not mechanistic evidence. The wins concentrate in the LEAST realistic regime (uniform family distribution, small B, heavy load) and the constant-service-time + token-count proxy removes the very feedback loop that determines whether matched-fairness survives on real hardware. Combined with thin per-cell n=6, no multiple-comparison correction across 92 cells, and the unresolved overlap with SGLang RadixAttention/vLLM APC scheduler (whose adjudication is load-bearing per the packet's own systems_reviewer note), this is a careful re-measurement with a fairness control — not yet evidence of a novel mechanism. YELLOW with concrete required_evidence for GPU pass: (1) real vLLM/SGLang APC scheduler comparison on wall-clock TTFT/throughput under bursty agentic Zipf-heavy traffic, (2) explicit comparison against SGLang cache-aware scheduling as a baseline (not just FCFS), (3) seeds>=20 with multiple-comparison-corrected CI on the win cells, (4) sensitivity curve on cache-size vs K (not 3 discrete points), (5) per-cell fairness-ratio distribution (not just mean) at matched-W, (6) test under realistic heavy-tailed family skew (Zipf s>=1.5) where the prior-art baselines are strongest.
